# Goal-level request to b2w_control's robot_mode_manager: "put the robot in this
# operation mode." The manager works out the legal Sport API sequence, enforces the
# interlocks, and verifies the result -- callers never touch SetRobotMode.
#
# This service returns as soon as the goal is ACCEPTED or REJECTED, not when the
# transition finishes: standing up takes seconds. Watch the manager's status topic
# (OperationModeStatus, ~/status -- /robot_mode_manager/status as launched today) for
# the outcome: `transitioning` goes false, and `last_error` is empty on success or
# carries the reason on failure. Gating on those two is more robust than watching
# `current`, which also reads as the goal value when the robot was already there.
#
# These constants are the single source of truth for operation-mode values --
# OperationModeStatus documents its fields as referring to them, and
# b2w_control/mode_fsm.hpp static_asserts its enum against them.
#
# Both drive modes are WHEELED -- a B2W drives on its wheels in either gait. What differs
# is the trade between straight-line speed and turning response.

uint8 LIE_DOWN=0        # down with the motors HELD -- not the same as damped. Settles in
                        # sport mode 7; mode 5 is only a ~1 s transient mid-descent
                        # (measured 2026-07-29, see docs/control-layer.md)
uint8 STAND=1           # up but hardware-immobile; Move is ignored (sport mode 6)
uint8 DRIVE_CRUISE=2    # gait 0, sport mode 14 -- faster straight line, dampened yaw
uint8 DRIVE_AGILE=3     # gait 1, sport mode 3  -- standard speed, responsive turning

# 4 RETIRED: RECOVER. It existed only to issue RecoveryStand (API 1006), which is an
#   aggressive self-right and has been removed from the stack -- see SetRobotMode. Getting
#   up from a damped robot now goes through the ordinary StandUp path like any other goal.
#   NEVER reuse this value: retired numbers stay retired so an old client cannot silently
#   mean something new.

uint8 mode
---
# accepted == false means nothing was sent to the robot. Common reasons:
#   unknown target, no fresh BaseControllerStatus, an uncharacterised sport mode,
#   the robot is moving (interlock), or a leg-reconfiguring command is still in flight.
bool accepted
string message
